  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "0.2.0\n- A connection's requests run side by side, so a slow file system call holds up only its own request (MaxConcurrentRequests, 64 by default). An ISmbFileSystem is therefore called from several requests at once; one open's calls still never overlap, and its READs and WRITEs keep the order they were sent in.\n- A request that takes long gets an interim STATUS_PENDING reply (InterimResponseDelay, half a second by default), so clients wait it out; a client's CANCEL fires the call's cancellation token.\n- A file system call that gives up with an OperationCanceledException fails its own command with STATUS_IO_TIMEOUT, also inside a compound chain, and the connection carries on. A CLOSE closes its file whatever becomes of the request.\n- READs and WRITEs carry 64 KiB. The megabyte offered before stalled a Mac's writes and could hang it.\n- A port a server listens on is its own. On Linux and macOS a second server on it started without an error and took connections; it is refused now.\n- A session's messages are signed on several processors at once.",
